    <string name="omni_help_first">First steps</string>
    <string name="omni_help_first_note">What this app is, and where to begin</string>
    <string name="omni_help_first_body">Omni Builder compiles Android packages on this device. There is no server and no desktop toolchain: the compiler, the packager and the signer all run here. Make a project from Home, write your code under Java, then build it.</string>
    <string name="omni_help_project">A new project</string>
    <string name="omni_help_project_note">Name, package, version and icon</string>
    <string name="omni_help_project_body">A project needs a name, a package name such as com.example.app, a version, and the Android versions it runs on. The package name is what Android knows an installed app by, so choose it before you publish rather than after. An icon can be added at any time from Files.</string>
    <string name="omni_help_write">Writing code</string>
    <string name="omni_help_write_note">The editor, and what it checks as you type</string>
    <string name="omni_help_write_body">Files opens a project. Tapping a source file opens the editor, which colours the code and checks it a moment after you stop typing. Names it already knows are offered as you type, and it will jump to where a name was written or list everywhere it is used.</string>
    <string name="omni_help_package">Building a package</string>
    <string name="omni_help_package_note">From sources to an APK and a bundle</string>
    <string name="omni_help_package_body">Build compiles the sources, packs the resources, writes the dex, seals the package and then verifies what it wrote. It produces an APK to install here and a bundle to upload. Every build needs a signing key, and a shared one is made for you the first time.</string>
    <string name="omni_help_sign">Signing</string>
    <string name="omni_help_sign_note">Keys, passwords, and what a signature proves</string>
    <string name="omni_help_sign_body">Every package is signed. A signature proves the package came from the key that signed it, and Android refuses an update signed by a different key. Keep your key and its password somewhere safe: a lost key cannot be replaced, and the app it signed can never be updated again.</string>
    <string name="omni_help_trouble">When something refuses</string>
    <string name="omni_help_trouble_note">Reading a refusal, and fixing what it names</string>
    <string name="omni_help_trouble_body">A refusal names a code, the file and the line it is about, and says what it wants instead. Project health lists what is wrong with a project before a build starts. Where the repair is one this app can make itself, it offers it and shows what it would change first.</string>
    <string name="omni_help_docs">Full documentation</string>
    <string name="omni_help_docs_note">Every screen, every refusal code</string>
    <string name="omni_help_docs_body">The refusal codes are documented inside the app itself. Every code an editor check or a build raises carries a sentence, in the language you chose, saying what it means and what to do about it.</string>
    <string name="omni_help_contact_note">If none of this answers it, write to us. Say what you did, what you expected, and what happened instead.</string>
    <string name="omni_help_signed">— Eaquel</string>
